**Sec. 90001**
This section appropriates $46.55 billion to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for fiscal year 2025, remaining available until September 30, 2029. These funds are designated for the construction, installation, or improvement of primary, waterborne, and secondary border barriers, as well as associated access roads. The appropriation also covers barrier system attributes like cameras, lights, and sensors, along with necessary ground preparation work to support Customs and Border Protection operations.

**Sec. 90002**
This section appropriates funds to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for personnel, bonuses, vehicles, and facilities. Specifically, it allocates $4.1 billion for hiring and training agents and support personnel, $2.05263 billion for recruitment, performance, and retention bonuses, $855 million for patrol vehicle repair and acquisition, and $5 billion for facility improvements. A restriction prohibits using these funds to recruit, hire, or train processing coordinators after October 31, 2028.

**Sec. 90003**
This section appropriates $45 billion to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to expand single adult alien detention capacity and family residential center capacity. It authorizes the detention of aliens in family residential centers pending removal decisions or until removal is completed, with detention standards for single adults set at the discretion of the Secretary of Homeland Security. The section defines "family residential center" as a DHS facility for detaining family units, including alien children who are not unaccompanied.

**Sec. 90004**
This section appropriates $6.168 billion to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for border security technology and screening enhancements. Funds are allocated for nonintrusive inspection equipment, air and marine platform upgrades, surveillance technology, biometric entry and exit systems, and screening of persons and unaccompanied alien children. The section restricts the use of funds for surveillance towers along the southwest and northern borders unless they have been tested and accepted by Customs and Border Protection for autonomous capabilities.

**Sec. 90005**
This section appropriates $2.575 billion to the Federal Emergency Management Agency for fiscal year 2025 for State Homeland Security Grant Programs, including $500 million for unmanned aircraft system threats, $625 million for the 2026 FIFA World Cup, $1 billion for the 2028 Olympics, and $450 million for Operation Stonegarden. It also establishes the State Border Security Reinforcement Fund with a $10 billion appropriation for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2034, for grants to states and local governments for border wall construction, ground preparation, interdiction, and alien relocation. Grants may cover expenses incurred on or after January 20, 2021, but do not authorize state or local governments to exercise federal immigration authorities.

**Sec. 90006**
This section appropriates $300 million to the Administrator of the Federal Emergency Management Agency for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to reimburse states and local agencies for extraordinary law enforcement costs associated with protecting presidential residences. Reimbursement is limited to costs incurred on or after July 1, 2024, that exceed typical operations, are directly attributable to Secret Service protection requests, and relate to nongovernmental properties designated under the Presidential Protection Assistance Act of 1976. FEMA may use up to 3 percent of these funds for administrative purposes.

**Sec. 90007**
This section appropriates $10 billion to the Secretary of Homeland Security for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029. These funds are designated for the reimbursement of costs incurred in undertaking activities that support the Department of Homeland Security's mission to safeguard the borders of the United States.

**Sec. 90101**
This section, titled the "FEHB Protection Act of 2025," requires the Director of the Office of Personnel Management to implement verification processes for qualifying life events and family member eligibility in the Federal Employees Health Benefits Program within one year. It mandates a comprehensive three-year audit of family member coverage starting one year after enactment, reviewing documents like marriage and birth certificates, and requires a disenrollment process for ineligible individuals within 180 days. The section amends 5 U.S.C. 8909 to set aside $66 million in fiscal year 2026 from program contributions, available until fiscal year 2035, to fund these oversight and audit activities.

**Sec. 90102**
This section appropriates $88 million for fiscal year 2026 to the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee to support oversight of the Coronavirus response and related funds, with the money remaining available until expended. It amends the CARES Act to include the reconciliation act titled "An Act to provide for reconciliation pursuant to title II of H. Con. Res. 14" in the list of covered legislation. Additionally, it extends the Committee's authority under the CARES Act from 2025 to 2034.

**Sec. 90103**
This section appropriates $100 million to the Office of Management and Budget for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029. These funds are designated for purposes of finding budget and accounting efficiencies within the executive branch. [end of text]


